From: rsvga
Subject: [PATCH] vmport: answer SVGA capability queries, minimal GuestRPC

VMware's SVGA drivers ask the hypervisor for the SVGA device
capabilities through the backdoor before they initialize the device
(BDOOR_CMD_GET_SVGA_CAPABILITIES, command 75, CPL 0 only; the type of
capability is in the high word of ECX, see SVGABackdoorCapType in
svga_reg.h). QEMU does not implement it, so the Windows WDDM driver
assumes a device without 3D and never uses guest-backed objects, command
buffers or screen targets, whatever the device reports.

Add opt-in properties to answer it: "svga-backdoor" enables the command,
"svga-caps", "svga-fifo-caps", "svga-3d-hwversion" and "svga-caps2"
give the answer for each type. On success EBX = BDOOR_MAGIC and EAX =
the value; other types are answered as unsupported (EAX = -1, EBX
unchanged). The values must be those of the SVGA device given to the
guest, typically an out-of-process device model that knows them.

The same drivers log through GuestRPC (BDOOR_CMD_MESSAGE, command 30)
and read their settings with "info-get guestinfo.*"; the VMware Tools
use GuestRPC both ways. Add an opt-in "guestrpc" property with a
minimal implementation of both channel kinds, with cookies and the
4-byte send and receive loops of open-vm-tools (no high-bandwidth port):

- RPCI (guest to host): messages are traced (vmport_guestrpc_message)
  and answered like a VMware host: success for "log", "guest.log.state",
  "tools.capability.*" and "tools.set.version", "0 Unknown command" for
  the rest. "info-get KEY" and "info-set KEY VALUE" use a small store,
  the guestinfo.* settings of a VMware VM: the "guestinfo" property sets
  initial values ("guestinfo.a=1;guestinfo.b=TRUE"), the guest can add
  up to 256 keys, unknown keys get "0 No value found". The VMware
  drivers read their options and log levels there.
- TCLO (host to guest): a new channel is greeted with "reset" then
  "Capabilities_Register", as on VMware (the VMware Tools only announce
  their capabilities on request); each guest message is the result of
  the previous command and gets the next queued command as its reply. The new QMP command
  x-vmport-guestrpc-send (unstable) queues a command for every TCLO
  channel, e.g. "Resolution_Set 1920 1080" for the VMware Tools.

Both are off by default: nothing changes for existing configurations.
User-mode guest programs such as the VMware Tools also need the KVM
module parameter enable_vmware_backdoor, and the Tools installer wants
VMware's CPUID signature (e.g. -cpu host,kvm=off,hv-relaxed,
hv-vendor-id=VMwareVMware).

Tested with QEMU 11.1.2, rsvga, the VMware SVGA WDDM driver 9.17.11
and VMware Tools 13.1.5 in Windows 11: the driver queries type 0 once
at start-up and logs through GuestRPC (more with
guestinfo.svga.wddm.miniportLogging=TRUE); the Tools register their
capabilities, and x-vmport-guestrpc-send "Resolution_Set 1600 900"
switches the guest to 1600x900.

